# Changelog Maintenance Rules

## General Guidelines

- Follow [Keep a Changelog](mdc:https:/keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0) format
- Always update the [CHANGELOG.md](mdc:CHANGELOG.md) when code changes are made
- Keep the changelog in strict reverse chronological order (newest entries at top)
- Use ISO 8601 date format (YYYY-MM-DD) for all entries
- Group related changes under appropriate section headers
- Use clear, concise language to describe changes
- Focus on user-facing changes and important technical improvements
- Reference issue/ticket numbers when applicable

- Keep minor changes and change details in files within the memory_bank folder. See the

## Version Numbering

- Use semantic versioning format: `2.00.xx` where xx is incremented by 1 for each day's entry
- When starting a new day, increment the patch version by 1 (e.g., 2.00.15 → 2.00.16)
- When reaching 2.00.99, talk to your user about cleaning up the repo
- Use minor version increments x.nn.xx where nn is the minor number, when there is an SDK breaking change.
- Use major version increments (3.00.00) only for major milestones and refactoring
- Consolidate all changes for a single day under one version number.

## Entry Structure

- Begin each entry with `## Version X.YY.ZZ - YYYY-MM-DD`
- Be sure you have the correct date - ask if the date seems very wrong compared to other changes.
- Organize changes into logical categories using level 3 headers (###)
- Common categories include:
  - Improvements
  - Bug Fixes
  - New Features
  - API Changes
  - Performance Improvements
  - Documentation
  - Database Improvements
  - UI Enhancements

## Entry Format

- Use bulleted lists (with `-`) for individual changes
- Start each bullet with a capitalized verb in past tense (Added, Fixed, Improved, Updated, etc.)
- Be specific about what changed and why, when relevant
- For complex changes, use nested bullets to provide additional details
- Use code formatting with backticks (`) for code references, files, and paths
- Keep entries focused on the what, not the how
- Limit each bullet to one logical change

## Entry Content

- Highlight changes visible to the end-user
- Mention significant internal architectural changes
- Include database schema changes
- Document API additions, changes, and deprecations
- Note performance improvements
- Reference UI changes and enhancements
- Include security fixes (without revealing exploitable details)
- Document changes to dependencies and third-party integrations

## Maintenance Practices

- Remove older entries only after proper archiving
- Maintain a "Previous Versions" section when condensing older entries
- Group previous versions by major feature categories
- Keep the file size manageable by archiving old entries periodically
- Update changelog before merging code to main branch
- Review changelog entries for clarity and completeness

## Example Entry

```markdown
## Version 2.0.15 - 2025-04-02

### Improvements

- Added Word and QCode Manager application with AWS database integration
- Implemented asset management system with classification and type support
- Added S3 integration for asset image storage with multiple resolution support
- Created user-friendly GUI with search, import/export, and thumbnail display
- Added comprehensive logging and error handling throughout the application

### Bug Fixes

- Fixed database connection issue when AWS credentials are missing
- Resolved UI freezing during large import operations
- Fixed thumbnail display scaling in asset browser
```

## Additional Guidelines

- Keep the main changelog file focused on user-relevant changes
- Maintain technical details in separate documentation
- Review changelog entries during code review process
- Ensure consistency in style and level of detail across entries
- Focus on impact rather than implementation details
- Make entries understandable to non-technical stakeholders when possible
